    "prompt": "The solution of the equation \\(7^{x+7}=8^x\\) can be expressed in the form \\(x=\\log_b 7^7.\\) What is \\(b?\\)"
  },
  "schemaVersion": "3.0",
  "solutions": [
    {
      "directNodeIds": [
        "math:real-exponent-laws",
        "cnm_h043"
      ],
      "explanation": "To identify the logarithm's base, rewrite the equation with a single quantity raised to \\(x\\). Since \\(7^x>0\\), division by \\(7^x\\) is valid and gives\n\\[\n7^7=\\frac{8^x}{7^x}=\\left(\\frac87\\right)^x.\n\\]\nBy definition, \\(a^x=N\\) is equivalent to \\(x=\\log_a N\\) when \\(a>0\\), \\(a\\ne1\\), and \\(N>0\\). Here these conditions hold, so\n\\[\nx=\\log_{8/7}(7^7).\n\\]\nThus \\(\\boxed{b=\\frac87}\\), choice \\(\\boxed{\\text{C}}\\).",
      "id": "rewrite-as-logarithm",
      "sourceRef": "amc/2010_AMC_12A_Q11#rewrite-as-logarithm",
      "title": "Use the definition of a logarithm"
    },
